{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["Let him go and return to his house — if his heart faints and he fears his mazal." "Lest he die in battle, etc. — for a man sets it in his heart when he goes out to battle and worries: Perhaps I have no mazal to enjoy my house, or my wife, or my vineyard, and therefore my mazal has caused me to go now to battle, and on account of this he is afraid to die. And he mentioned three things: house, wife, and vineyard. And in conclusion he includes all things: מי האיש הירא ורך הלבב — whether in those things we have mentioned or in other things. And so in Kohelet he counts three things and goes back and includes them all: מה יתרון לאדם. It is indeed diminished from everything, for: דור הולך ודור אחר בא — the sons are not in the likeness of their fathers. And this is not a remembrance, but והארץ לעולם עומדת — as in the six days of Genesis, and likewise: השמש — and he goes back and includes all things, יגעים — all the deeds of the world are thus all of them, like the six days of Genesis, but it would be toil and effort to enumerate them all. ולא יוכל איש לדבר ולא תמלא אזן משמוע — if one were to recount to him; rather, this is the principle: אין כל חדש — and all of them have a remembrance, but אין זכרון לראשונים — for the one born is not like the one who gives birth."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {1 "The passage cites Kohelet extensively; all biblical quotations are left unaddressed as in the source. The final clause translating ולא יוכל איש לדבר ולא תמלא אזן משמוע follows the syntax of Rashbam's sentence, which embeds the verse quotation mid-thought. On 'mazal' as astrological fortune/luck, Rashbam uses the term in its medieval sense."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:39:17.860607746Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}